{% if feedback_is_check %}
Review the changes with `git diff {{ base }}...HEAD` in the workspace. Verify the repair and its
tests against the evidence. The `replies` required by the report are internal explanations for
{{ feedback_source_ids | tojson }} and will not be posted. Escalate with the actual cause and
evidence if the failure is demonstrably outside the repository's control. Accept only a verified
fix, and supply a whole-change `commit_subject` and a fresh pull request description. Do not change
code or tests.
{% else %}
Review the changes with `git diff {{ base }}...HEAD` in the workspace. The facts above outrank the
Implementer's claims. Check that each accepted request is really satisfied, that each
`already_addressed` claim is true, and that each reply is accurate against the snapshot and the
Git and check evidence. Do not change code or tests.

In a discussion, the messages marked `"new": true` are the ones each reply must answer; the
unmarked ones are context, including earlier answers.

A `clarify` reply must be a specific question that asks only for the information its `detail`
names; it must not claim any change or promise work. Choose `accept` only when every change and
reply is right; give the final reply for each source item that has one in `replies`. Check the
target project's instructions and documentation for its commit-message convention. On `accept`, also give `commit_subject`: a concise subject for the whole
change following that convention, or Conventional Commits if none is documented (no task reference).
On acceptance, write `pr_description` anew for the entire final change, for a reader unfamiliar
with the task. Start with a one-line summary, then short headed sections for what changed and why,
effects (including user behavior, compatibility and migrations), checks including what was not
checked, risks or follow-ups, and a reference to the task. Omit empty sections and use bullets
when useful. Use the change and verification evidence; do not invent facts or reuse the short
review summary. Do this even when the feedback round made no code changes.
Choose `changes_requested` with `issues` for further rework, or `escalate` with options for the
Operator. `supersede` is not available.
{% endif %}
